  "full-article-text": "<p id=\"\">A utility token is a type of cryptographic <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-a-token\"><strong id=\"\">token</strong></a> that can be redeemed or used as part of a service provided by the issuer of the token; hence, it has “utility.”&nbsp;</p><p id=\"\">For example, <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-ethereum\"><strong id=\"\">Ethereum’s</strong></a> ETH is often considered a utility token, as ETH owners can use ETH to pay for <a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-gas-in-ethereum\"><strong id=\"\">transaction</strong> <strong id=\"\">fees</strong></a> on the Ethereum network. Likewise, Filecoin’s FIL is also widely considered a utility token, as FIL is used to pay for file storage on the Filecoin network.</p><p id=\"\"><strong id=\"\">Further reading:</strong></p><ul id=\"\"><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-a-governance-token\"><strong id=\"\">What is a Governance Token?</strong></a></li><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-an-nft\"><strong id=\"\">What is an NFT?</strong></a></li><li id=\"\"><a id=\"\" href=\"https://learn.radixdlt.com/article/what-is-defi-2\"><strong id=\"\">What is DeFi?</strong></a></li></ul>",
